From b5f069d698bd2bb927a2135254eb5242cd96de01 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <a@b.c>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 14:09:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/6] chl: size-bucketed pool of texture memory
 (CHL_MEMPOOL_MB)

CoreAnimation sizes its offscreen targets to the moving layer extent, so the exact-shape texture pool in
bgmtl.m hits only 30-45 % (NOTES). What a texture costs the kernel is its BO (vkAllocateMemory), not the
VkImage or its views. chl_texture_create now takes memory from a pool of destroyed textures' blocks kept by
size: blocks are allocated rounded to eighths of a power of two (at least 64 KB), a new image binds the
smallest block that is big enough and at most 25 % + 64 KB too big, at offset 0 (an image may be bound to
memory that held another one; its layout starts UNDEFINED). The block goes back to the pool when the
texture is destroyed (command buffers hold the texture until they complete, so the GPU is done with it).

- air/chl_mempool.h: the pool as plain C over opaque handles; test/chl_mempool_test.c exercises it on a
  host (bucket rounding, owner separation, smallest fit, cap eviction, expiry, slot bound). Run with gcc.
- Off unless CHL_MEMPOOL_MB is set (cap in MB; the inject should set it for backboardd next to
  BGMTL_TEXPOOL_MB). CHL_MEMPOOL_AGE_MS (default 5000) is how long an unused block stays.
- chl_trim() frees the pool (for the idle trim); chl_device_close drains the device's blocks.
- chl_stats gains pool_hits / pool_misses / pool_held_bytes.
- Imported IOSurface textures, views and buffers are never pooled.

Risk (guess, not tested): Turnip UBWC flag data inside a reused block is stale at bind time. The spec allows
aliasing in UNDEFINED layout and Turnip handles the UNDEFINED transition, but this must be seen on the
phone (swipe test, look for corrupted blur targets). Unset CHL_MEMPOOL_MB to turn it off.

chl_vk.c was not compiled (needs Mesa headers + iOS).

Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LbQpVTcCZP2zgEqnwYHZKn
